* stands out as a solid developing move that doesn’t commit the queen prematurely, increases central influence, prepares for flexible middlegame plans (like ...e5 or ...d5), and avoids tactical exposure.\n\nReason:  \nDeveloping minor pieces efficiently is critical at this stage. The knight on c6 enhances control over the center, supports future pawn breaks, and connects well with the rest of Black’s army. Unlike queen moves, it doesn’t risk losing tempo or getting harassed. It’s a principled, low-risk, high-reward choice in line with opening principles.\n\nAnswer: N: b8-c6"}
