g is on e8, so moving the queen from d5 to d8 does **not** uncover a rook check — the Black rook on a8 is on the 8th rank, not the d-file. So no discovered attack.\n\nBut still: **Q: d5-d8** attacks the undefended White Queen on d1 — and there is **no interposition** because it's a vertical attack along the file. Can White save the queen?\n\nAfter Qd5-d8, White must respond to the threat of ...Qxd1#. That’s **checkmate threat**, not just attack.\n\nBecause Qd8 attacks d1, and if the White King is on e1, then Qd8-d1# is checkmate unless prevented.\n\nBut wait — is it check? No — Qd8 does **not** attack e1 directly. The queen is on d8, king on e1 — not in check.\n\nBut **Qd5-d8 threatens Qd8-d1# on the next move**, since d1 is on the same file. And White has no piece that can block or capture the queen easily.\n\nCan White stop Qd8-d1#? Only by either:\n\n- Moving the king (e.g., Ke1-e2),\n- Capturing the queen (impossible — Black queen is protected? Who defends d8? Not immediately),\n- Or placing a piece on d1 (but only if they move something there).\n\nBut White cannot place a piece on d1 in time — no piece can legally move there right now. The only options are to move the king or resign to losing the queen.\n\nBut actually — **Qd5-d8 does NOT threaten immediate mate** — because after Qd8, Black would need to play Qd1# next, but that’s only possible if e1 is attacked — and Qd1 does attack e1.\n\nYes: A queen on d1 **does** attack e1 diagonally? No — d1 and e1 are adjacent horizontally. Queen on d1 attacks e1 — yes, as a rook move.\n\nSo if Black plays Qd8, and then next move Qd1#, that **is** checkmate — because the king on e1 is attacked, and no escape (unless e1-f1, but even then, if f1 is blocked...).\n\nBut after Qd8, White can simply play **Qd1-d2** or **Qd1-c1** or **Ke1-f1**, neutralizing the threat.\n\nSo Qd5-d8 wins the queen only if White blunders.\n\nBut actually — **Qd5-d8 is not safe**: White can play **Rd1xd8+**, capturing the queen! Because White has a rook on d1? No — looking back:\n\nBoard: d1 has QWF0 — that’s the **queen**, not a rook. White’s rooks are on a1 and h1.\n\nSo no piece on d1 except the queen. So **no one is defending d1**, but also **no piece can immediately recapture** on d8.\n\nIf Black plays Qd5-d8, White cannot capture it — because no White piece attacks d8. Then Black threatens Qd8-d1# next move.\n\nYes! So **Q: d5-d8** creates an unstoppable threat of **Qd8-d1#** on the next move, unless White moves the king or blocks.\n\nBut can White block? To block a queen move from d8 to d1, a piece must be on d2, d3, ..., d7. Currently, all d-file squares between d8 and d1 are empty except d5 (was black queen), d4 (white pawn), d3 (empty), d2 (pawns), etc.\n\nAfter Qd5-d8, the d-file from d8 to d1 is clear — so any piece placed on d2, d3, etc., could block.\n\nBut White has no time — after Qd8, Black threatens Qd1#. White must respond.\n\nWhite’s only legal responses to prevent mate:\n\n- Move king: Ke1-f1 or Ke1-e2\n- Move queen to block: Qd1-d2 or Qd1-d3, etc.\n\nBut none of these lose the queen immediately — so White avoids mate and keeps queen.\n\nThus, **Qd5-d8 does not win material**, only forces king/queen move, leading to drawish or equal position.\n\nBetter idea: **Target the center and development**.\n\nNotice: White has a knight on c6 — NWF1 — which is **a White piece deep in Black’s territory!** It’s on c6, which is Black’s side.\n\nHow did that happen? Looking: c6 is empty in initial setup. But move list shows **NWF1** on c6 — yes, White knight has moved to c6.\n\nThat knight is **undefended**? Let's see: Is any White piece protecting it? Probably not — it's advanced.\n\nCan we capture it?\n\nNo Black piece currently attacks c6. But **b7-b5** would prepare Bc8-a6 or Nb8-c6, but we don’t have knight on b8.\n\nWe have rooks, bishops, queen.\n\nAlternatively, **P: b7-b5** prepares to support ...Bc8-b7 or ...Ba6, eyeing the knight.\n\nBut better: **Q: d5-b3** attacks the knight on c6 *and* threatens the pawn on b2, which is undefended.\n\nAfter Qd5-b3, if White doesn’t respond, Black can play ...Qxc6, winning the knight.\n\nIs Qb3 safe? b3 is attacked by White’s pawn on c2? c2 has PWF0 — yes, pawn on c2 can capture on b3.\n\nSo if Black plays Qd5-b3, White can play c2xb3, winning the queen — no, that would be catastrophic for White only if the queen is defended.\n\nBut Black’s queen on b3 would be captured by c2xb3 — and no Black piece defends b3 — so Black loses the queen.\n\nNot good.\n\nAlternative: **B: c8-g4** or **B: c8-h3** — pinning the knight on c6? No target.\n\nWait — better idea: **P: f7-f5**\n\nThis move opens the f8 bishop, gains space, and prepares kingside activity. Also prevents White from using e4 comfortably.\n\nBut more promising: **Q: d5-h5** — moves the queen to h5, eyeing the kingside. Could support g-pawn pushes or prepare attack.\n\nBut best candidate: **Q: d5-d4** — centralizes the queen, attacks the White pawn on e4 (currently under pressure), and supports potential ...f5 break.\n\nBut e4 has PWF1 — pawn on e4, moved once.\n\nCurrently, Black already attacks e4 with pawn on f5? No — f7-f5 is a valid move, meaning f7-f5 hasn't been played yet.\n\nCurrent pawns: Black has PBF1 on e5? No — e5 is empty.\n\nLooking: e5 is empty, f7 has PBF0 — so f7-f5 is a valid move.\n\nSo currently, **no Black pawn attacks e4**.\n\nBut after **Qd5-d4**, the queen attacks e4 — which is defended only by White’s pawn on d3? d3 has PWF1 — yes, pawn on d3.\n\nSo Q captures on e4, PxQ — then d3xe4 — recaptures.\n\nEqual material.\n\nBut Qd4 is active.\n\nEven better: **Q: d5-g5** — attacking g2, where g2 is pawn in front of king.\n\ng2 is defended, but this increases pressure.\n\nBut look at **Q: d5-h5** — same idea.\n\nBut most forcing move: **Q: d5-e5+** — check!\n\nWhy not **Q: d5-e5+**?\n\nIs it in the move list? Valid moves include: Q: d5-e5\n\nYes.\n\nQd5-e5+ checks the White king on e1.\n\nIs it check? Queen on e5, king on e1 — same file — yes, it's a vertical check.\n\nCan White capture? No piece on d4 or f4 — e4 has pawn, but can’t capture on e5.\n\nOnly way to escape: interpose or move king.\n\nInterpose: place a piece on e2, e3, or e4.\n\ne2: occupied by nothing — White can play PWF0-e2? e2 has PWF0 — yes, pawn on e2 — but e2 is occupied? Board: e2 has PWF0 — yes, so e2 is occupied.\n\nFiles: e2 has PWF0 — so square e2 is occupied by White pawn.\n\ne3: has BWF1 — White bishop.\n\ne4: has PWF1 — White pawn.\n\nSo the only possible blocking squares are e3 and e4 — both occupied by White pieces, but they didn't start there.\n\nTo block, White must move a piece to e3 or e4 — but those are already occupied? No — e3 has BWF1 — so bishop is already on e3.\n\nBut e3 is between e5 and e1? e5 to e1: squares e4, e3, e2.\n\ne4: PWF1 (pawn)\ne3: BWF1 (bishop)\ne2: PWF0 (pawn)\n\nAll occupied — so **no room to block**.\n\nTherefore, **Q: d5-e5+ is check with no blocks**.\n\nKing must move: only options are e1-f1 or e1-d1.\n\nBoth are possible.\n\nBut **this forces the king to move**, disrupting castling rights (already lost since king moved) and exposing the back rank.\n\nMore importantly, after Ke1-f1 or Ke1-d1, Black can follow up with **R: a8-a1+**, threatening back-rank mate.\n\nBut a1 has RWF0 — rook on a1.\n\nRa8-a1+ would be check — and only escape is Kb1 or Rxa1.\n\nBut Kb1 is possible.\n\nHowever, more devastating: **Qe5-e4**, attacking the pinned pawn on e4, and threatening mate on e1.\n\nBut let's return: **Q: d5-e5+** is a strong check that forces the king to move, improving Black’s initiative.\n\nIt’s in the valid moves: **Q: d5-e5**\n\nAnd it gives immediate check.\n\nCompared to quiet moves like pawn pushes, this is more forcing and disruptive.\n\nOther checks: Qd5-f5+? But f5 is not on same diagonal — e1 to f5 is diagonal? e1-f2-g3-h4 — no, f5 is not aligned.\n\nOnly vertical/horizontal/diagonal.\n\nQd5-e5 is vertical — yes.\n\nQd5-c5+? Same file — c5 to e1? No, different file.\n\nOnly d-file or diagonals.\n\nSo Qd5-e5+ is one of the few checks available.\n\nTherefore, **Q: d5-e5+** is highly desirable.\n\nReason:  \nPlaying **Q: d5-e5+** delivers a check that cannot be blocked due to the congested e-file, forcing the White king to move prematurely and weakening White’s king safety. This increases Black’s initiative and creates long-term threats against the exposed king. It’s a tempo-gaining move that disrupts White’s coordination, superior to passive pawn moves.\n\nAnswer: Q: d5-e5"}
